INT. BOWLING ALLEY - LANE 12 - NIGHT

Championship night. Cigarette haze under the neon. A scoreboard overhead: Ninth frame. Tied, 218 to 218.

COACH ARTIE KOWALCZYK, 60s, sweat-stained cardigan, stands behind the bench chewing a toothpick down to nothing. At the approach, RUBY DELGADO, 20s, steps up with a pink ball, wipes her palm on a towel, never meeting his eyes. Behind her, TEDDY BRIGHT, 19, rubs his bad shoulder against the rail. DEVON WALLACE, 30s, the anchor, folds his arms and watches the pins. Two rows back on the bench, the tiny MRS. FUJIMOTO, 70s, knits a scarf, needles clicking steadily. At the far lane, rival captain GIL SANTORO, 40s, grins and spins a ball on one finger.

                    ARTIE
Spare, Ruby. That's all we need.

Ruby nods. Takes her stance. Behind her, Teddy stops rubbing his shoulder. Devon unfolds his arms. Mrs. Fujimoto drops a stitch.

She releases. The ball rolls down the oil, swerving left, then straight. Pins explode. Nine.

One left. The ten pin wobbles. Everybody holds their breath.

It stands.

Ruby turns slowly. Walks back to the bench. Sits beside Mrs. Fujimoto. Picks up her towel. Folds it into a perfect square.

                    ARTIE
That's okay.

Devon takes the pink ball from the rack. Weighs it. Hands it back to her.

                    DEVON
Again.

                    RUBY
It's not my turn.

                    DEVON
I know.

He walks to the approach himself.
